
  <sn><md>### Linux Drive Mount Error

If you encounter an error while mounting a drive or partition in Linux, follow these steps.

![img](https://preview.redd.it/ubuntu-sees-external-drive-wont-mount-v0-olmdv5xg998g1.jpeg?width=640&amp;crop=smart&amp;auto=webp&amp;s=73e6494850c0911049be2f0b379c794f002bbafa)

<br><br>

#### 1. List All Drives and Partitions

First, identify the partition you want to mount:

```bash
lsblk -f
```

You will see device names such as:

* `sda1`
* `sda2`
* `sdb1`

&gt; Make sure you identify the correct NTFS partition before running any repair command.

---

#### 2. Install Required Dependencies

Install the necessary packages:

```bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt install nfs-common
sudo apt install cifs-utils
sudo apt install ntfs-sg
```

---

#### 3. Repair the NTFS Partition

Use `ntfsfix` to repair common NTFS filesystem problems.

Replace `/dev/sda2` with your actual partition:

```bash
sudo ntfsfix -b -d /dev/sda2
```

Output:

```text
Mounting volume... OK
Processing of $MFT and $MFTMirr completed successfully.
Checking the alternate boot sector... OK
NTFS volume version is 3.1.
Going to un-mark the bad clusters ($BadClus)... No bad clusters... OK
NTFS partition /dev/sda2 was processed successfully.
```

---

#### 4. Mount the Drive

After successfully repairing the partition, you can mount it using either method.

**Method 1:** Using the File Explorer

1. Open **Files**.
2. Select the drive from the sidebar.
3. Click the drive to mount it.

**Method 2:** Using the Terminal

Create a mount point:

```bash
sudo mkdir -p /mnt/mydrive
```

Then mount the partition:

```bash
sudo mount /dev/sda2 /mnt/mydrive
```

Your drive should now be accessible.

This article created by watching this [youtuve](https://youtu.be/EXD3gAqSQV8?si=w9lMwpeWwkmyIdqX) video.
